WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.079 --> 00:00:08.070
Gusts of cool wind rush through a wide valley. A herd of cattle ahead of him, a cowboy rides steadily across the plains.

2
00:00:08.071 --> 00:00:16.003
He sits astride his horse in a wide-brimmed hat, a fringed shirt, jeans, and pointed cowboy boots, enjoying the sunset.

3
00:00:16.004 --> 00:00:19.066
Soon he will arrive at the ranch he calls home.

4
00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:26.447
This is the classic image of the cowboy—a vision of strength and bravery that has left its mark on the Western imagination.

5
00:00:26.448 --> 00:00:34.209
The cowboys portrayed on television and the movie screen, however, are quite different from the original cowboys of the Wild West.

6
00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:42.106
Cowboys, in the traditional sense, are people who tend to the animals on a farm and drive them across large tracts of open land.

7
00:00:42.107 --> 00:00:53.921
They use lassos, horses, and dogs to herd livestock for ranchers, rounding up a few animals at the end of the day or guiding thousands of head of cattle miles across the country.

8
00:00:53.922 --> 00:01:00.198
On these great "cattle drives," cowboys lead the herds to the cattle markets where they will be sold.

9
00:01:00.199 --> 00:01:08.561
Cowboys are also trained to take care of other animals, such as sheep, pigs, and chickens, and perform many tasks around the farm.

10
00:01:08.562 --> 00:01:17.894
Societies all over the world have people who take care of animals, but the modern cowboy descends from the Spanish tradition of the sixteenth century.

11
00:01:17.895 --> 00:01:25.616
Much of Spain is dry and mountainous, with sparse vegetation, so cattle needed to roam widely to get enough to eat.

12
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:29.779
The Spanish cowboys tended these wide-ranging herds on horseback.

13
00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:39.876
When the Spanish conquistadors came to the New World, they brought horses, which had been extinct in the Western Hemisphere since the end of the last ice age.

14
00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:47.440
The horses aided the Spanish in the settlement of New Spain, which later became Mexico and the southwestern United States.

15
00:01:47.442 --> 00:01:59.754
The Mexican-American War (1846–1848) expanded the United States' territory into Mexico and helped to bring the cowboy way of life northward into the United States.

16
00:01:59.755 --> 00:02:09.559
After the American Civil War, which lasted from 1861 to 1865, the cowboy tradition continued to travel north into Canada.

17
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:15.693
Although history books rarely mention female cowboys, known as "cowgirls," they did exist.

18
00:02:15.694 --> 00:02:19.896
Many women worked on ranches, doing every possible task.

19
00:02:19.897 --> 00:02:26.012
They even ran family operations, which was unusual in other sectors of society for the time.

20
00:02:26.013 --> 00:02:33.959
Split skirts, which resemble loose pants, were invented so that women were not hampered by their clothing, especially on horseback.

21
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:45.765
But since riding astride was considered unladylike, many women chose to use sidesaddles that had been adapted for ranch work and allowed women to ride while wearing conventional female attire.

22
00:02:45.766 --> 00:02:51.481
Advances in technology brought the cowboy era to a close at the end of the nineteenth century.

23
00:02:51.482 --> 00:02:58.814
Barbed wire was invented in the 1870s and used to construct inexpensive fences around farms and pasture.

24
00:02:58.815 --> 00:03:05.020
The wire helped ranchers control their cattle, with fewer men on horseback needed to watch the herds.

25
00:03:05.021 --> 00:03:12.702
Unfortunately, the ranchers overgrazed their land, leading to widespread starvation of their herds in the decade that followed.

26
00:03:12.703 --> 00:03:24.850
The expansion of railroads in the late 1890s and the invention of the cattle truck in the 1940s made long cattle drives across the nation—and many cowboys—largely unnecessary.

27
00:03:24.851 --> 00:03:34.288
As work on farms became scarcer, cowboys and cowgirls turned to other ways of making a living; many began to compete in rodeos.

28
00:03:34.289 --> 00:03:39.463
The word "rodeo" comes from a Spanish word for the process of rounding up cattle.

29
00:03:39.464 --> 00:03:46.659
In the 1880s, a sport grew out of this practice, incorporating influences from Spanish bullfights.

30
00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:55.597
The events that make up a rodeo have changed little since that time and include trick roping, calf roping, and bull riding, among others.

31
00:03:55.598 --> 00:04:08.643
The Rodeo Association of America was formed partly to organize the many rodeo competitions across the country, but also to create a point system to fairly judge participants' performances.

32
00:04:08.644 --> 00:04:18.304
Cowgirls began to increasingly participate in rodeos in the early 1900s, when the Wild West show, a large, traveling rodeo, became popular.

33
00:04:18.305 --> 00:04:22.534
However, fewer events were organized for women than for men.

34
00:04:22.535 --> 00:04:36.115
Several organizations, such as the National Cowgirl Museum and Hall of Fame and the National Women's Rodeo Association, now exist to promote cowgirls and recognize the accomplishments of the women of the American West.

35
00:04:36.116 --> 00:04:42.837
The number of cowboys and cowgirls is declining. Our fascination with them, however, continues to grow.

36
00:04:42.838 --> 00:04:47.607
Popular culture has heavily romanticized the cowboy's solitary lifestyle.

37
00:04:47.608 --> 00:04:57.611
Cowboys are often featured in books and movies, particularly those from the 1930s to the 1960s, when the genre known as the "western" was in its heyday.

38
00:04:57.612 --> 00:05:01.816
These portrayed the cowboy as a seminomadic outlaw.

39
00:05:01.817 --> 00:05:08.213
As a result, many people believe the stereotype that cowboys are irresponsible and violent loners.

40
00:05:08.214 --> 00:05:16.266
The term "cowboy" is often used pejoratively to describe someone who has no respect for authority and an inclination toward lawlessness.

41
00:05:16.267 --> 00:05:23.750
But there are qualities that cowboys are admired for as well: their self-reliance, courage, and willingness to work hard.

42
00:05:23.751 --> 00:05:30.916
For this reason, the cowboy has become a widely accepted symbol of American individualism and independence.

43
00:05:30.917 --> 00:05:39.464
The National Football League honored all cowboys when they created a new team called the Cowboys in Irving, Texas, a suburb of Dallas.

44
00:05:39.465 --> 00:05:45.964
The cowboy's style of dress has also been incorporated into popular culture, with a few changes.

45
00:05:45.965 --> 00:05:54.285
Traditionally, cowboys did not wear fancy clothing. They were often paid very little and wore whatever hand-me-downs they could find.

46
00:05:54.286 --> 00:06:06.109
Wide-brimmed cowboy hats, which are now internationally popular, were worn merely to shield their eyes from the sun, and pointed boots with heels were worn to make it easier for their feet to stay in the stirrups.

47
00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:12.432
People now wear cowboy hats and boots with everything from skirts and jeans to business attire.

48
00:06:12.433 --> 00:06:22.823
As of 2003, there were 9,730 working cowboys in the United States, of which 3,290 were rodeo workers.

49
00:06:22.824 --> 00:06:28.085
The duties of these modern cowboys have changed dramatically since the sixteenth century.

50
00:06:28.086 --> 00:06:39.631
They now control many different types of farm equipment, including tractors and other vehicles designed for agricultural work, but much of their time is still spent on horseback, riding on the plains.

51
00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:45.585
Today's rodeo cowboys still perform their acts in the style of the cowboys on the Spanish frontier.

52
00:06:45.586 --> 00:06:52.659
The cowboy way of life has seen many changes, but as long as there are farms and rodeos, it will endure.

